Z||000024||Lady Pegasus||11-18-2001||09:30 PM||||The drink ouring came to mind, but 87 really like's his Cherry Coke.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Anyhow.  I don't know.  I don't ever plan on spanking my children.  My mom did a pretty fair job.  But then again, I did go to parochial school and was practically raised in the church (perfect Sunday School attendance EVERY year.  Oh yeah).  So maybe all that had something to do with it.  I can tell you honestly that if I had been sent to the public school for grade school, I wouldn't have turned out the way I have.  Now I know that not EVERYONE can be sent to private school, but I know that when I have children, I don't care if I have to work two full-time jobs, my kids will go to private school.  AND NEVER DAY-CARE!!  (Unless 87 gets a cushy job and I can just sit on my a$$ eating bon-bon's all day!!)  Don't read that, honey!!  <BR>P.S.  I LOVE POE!!  Sorry.<P>------------------<BR>Hey pretty....don't you wanna take a ride with me...through my world?||||reg||6
Z||000025||Raydar||11-19-2001||10:54 AM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Lady Pegasus:<BR><B>...I did go to parochial school and was practically raised in the church (perfect Sunday School attendance EVERY year.  Oh yeah).  So maybe all that had something to do with it.  I can tell you honestly that if I had been sent to the public school for grade school, I wouldn't have turned out the way I have...<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I second that. <BR>I survived catholic school. (Yes. The horror stories are true.) Although it probably has a lot to do with why I don't go to church now, I think it helped me to be a better citizen. Kept me under control until I figured out what life was about. <BR>I'm not sure how I would have turned out, otherwise. (As it was, there was one teacher who <I>just knew</I> that I was going to end up in prison.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"> ) <BR>I found out early that a good healthy dose of fear can sometimes be an acceptable substitute for respect and common sense.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 11-19-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000026||isthiswhereiputausername?||11-19-2001||11:24 AM||||I agree with you completetly Lady P..   We have had a few annoying kids at the theatres up here in Canada and last time I was in wichita, we went to see a movie, SAME THING.. 6-7 kids about the ages of 17-20 sitting behind us. I told the kid off and he cowered in his seat.. (maybe it was the look on my face hoping he would mouth off  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">) <P>If the movie is gonna be really good,we will go and see it and hope everyones quiet, but otherwise we stay at home and watch the VCD of it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000027||Mach10||11-19-2001||11:32 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>   I second that. <BR>I survived catholic school. (Yes. The horror stories are true.) Although it probably has a lot to do with why I don't go to church now, I think it helped me to be a better citizen. Kept me under control until I figured out what life was about. <BR>I'm not sure how I would have turned out, otherwise. (As it was, there was one teacher who <I>just knew</I> that I was going to end up in prison.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"> ) <BR>I found out early that a good healthy dose of fear can sometimes be an acceptable substitute for respect and common sense.     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>wish i could say the same thing. i came out of catholic school damaged. the horror stories are true. hipocracy reins supreme... of course, they *could* be right and it was all my fault for being different (imagine being told this at age 13 by the principal).<P>it's not the school you go to, or the church you go to. i think that parenting is a case-by-case thing. i know plenty of children (myself included) that benefitted from the occasional smack upside the head when we deserved it. i also know of plenty of parents that are willing to use spankings <B>but never have to</B>. if the child manages to learn right from wrong in his/her own way on his/her own terms and is able to apply it then there's no need for physical correction now is there?<BR>||||reg||7
